# {{ names.add_project }} (maintainer authoring helper)

Create / update a per-project doc in **{{ names.knowledge }}**. The authority is the
shared `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md` — this skill is the working kit: copy a template, draft,
run the checklist, finalize.

Input is `$ARGUMENTS`: a repo name, or a path to a note to distill. If `references/project/<repo-name>.md` already exists this is an **update**: edit it in place, never a second doc.

## Target paths

```
KNOWLEDGE={{ derived.knowledge_root }}   # adjust to your clone
$KNOWLEDGE/references/project/<repo-name>.md   # the doc you write
$KNOWLEDGE/references/project/index.md          # nav table — Owner + Audience columns MANDATORY
```

Full path set (`SKILL.md` / `references/domain/`{{#if has.observability_file}} / `observability.md`{{/if}}), the `$KNOWLEDGE` root, and all conventions: `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md` (source of truth — read it), plus the rules specific to {{ identity.display_name }} in `../{{ names.local_rules }}.md`. Edit the source repository, never an installed cache copy.

## Working from existing notes

When the input is a note (personal doc / wiki page / old write-up), treat it as a **lead, not truth** — may be outdated, wrong, or unimportant. Distill, don't transcribe:

1. **Verify against the project's repo first** — for anything code-checkable, Explore the doc's own repo (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §4`) and write what the **code** says; the note only points where to look. Track **CONFIRMED / NOTE-ERROR (note said X, code says Y) / NOT-FOUND**, correct in the doc, tell the user.
2. **Pick the granularity** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §0`): keep decision-grade (cross-repo relations, env/domains, deploy topology, gotchas, who-to-find). **DROP** package lists, class/folder dumps, full config — anything `grep`-able in 10 min. Big detail → point to source. "My note had it" is not a reason to keep it.
3. **Wikis and external pages = reference only, often stale** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`): code is the source of truth. Mark links "reference (may be stale)".
4. **Right home** — if the note is actually a **cross-project flow / business rule**, it belongs in a domain doc → use `{{ names.add_domain }}`, not a project doc (a project doc = one repo's basics). Conversely keep single-repo specifics here.
5. **Big notes → batches**: process a section at a time; report "kept / dropped / verified / TODO" before writing, let the user correct.

## Flow

1. **Scope + ownership** — the project's **own** repo is under `{{ paths.repos_root }}` (a project doc = that one repo's basics; not there → clone it, or write a domain doc for the cross-project facts instead). **Per-fact routing, not per-role** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`): probe `ls {{ paths.repos_root }}` for the **cross-referenced** repos (Dependencies / Consumed by / Flows) — name upstreams you don't have (Dependencies lists by name, no clone needed); a Flows link to an uncloned **our-side** project → tag `(unverified — <missing repo>)`; a fact owned by **another team's** code/settings we never clone or reach → `(external: <team>)`; code-invisible facts → `(human:)`. {{#if has.live_tools}}Preflight the live tools step 3 names before researching, as `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §5` ("Preflight") says. {{/if}}**Owner** per `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`: {{#if has.owner_source}}read {{ scope.owner_source }} in the project's repo and map it onto the vocabulary; no value there → `unknown`{{else}}`unknown` until the user decides, offered as a proposal in step 6's question{{/if}}.
2. **Research** — fan out an Explore agent per `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §4` (decision-grade facts + `file:line`, "not found" over guessing). A source the repo lacks (README, CI file, deploy manifest, tests) is a fact about the project: a structured field takes `none` and prose leaves it out, and it is never a reason to ask the user. Purpose comes from what the code shows (entry points, routes, consumers, jobs, manifest description), never from the repo name; the business reason is `(human:)` or left out.
3. **Verify with live tools** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §5`):{{#if has.live_tools}}
{{#if has.logs}}
   - log identity via a live query on {{ derived.labels.logs }}, never read from config. If the tool is unreachable, write one `(unverified — <tool> unreachable)` on the fact.
{{/if}}
{{#if has.global_settings}}
   - settings namespace via {{ capabilities.global_settings.lookup }}.
{{/if}}
{{#if has.external_reference}}
   - values whose source of truth is the external reference ({{ derived.labels.external_reference }}) are read from it, not derived from code.
{{/if}}
{{else}} no live tools are configured for this knowledge base — verify from code only.
{{/if}}
4. **Pick a template** (table below) → copy from `templates/`. An existing doc keeps its shape: edit it in place and re-verify every claim you touch.
5. **Draft** — fill the body prose in English; **section headings stay fixed** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §7`). Set `Last updated` to today. Move `Verified at` to the verified commit only when the **whole** doc was re-verified against it (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §3`); after a partial update leave it, so the audit still diffs the unreviewed range. Project docs cite symbols inline; add an `<!-- anchors -->` block when the project is cross-repo or multi-deployment, and not otherwise (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §9` defines both).
6. **Run `CHECKLIST.md`** against the draft; ask the open points ({{#if has.owner_source}}anything no source holds, except an owner the source lacks, which stays `unknown`{{else}}ownership proposals, anything no source holds{{/if}}) as one question with a proposal each, and one question for the whole run when several projects were written. When nobody can answer (a run with no user in the loop), the open points go in the closing report and the affected fields stay `unknown` or marked.
7. **Update `references/project/index.md`** — add a row `| Project | Owner | Audience | one-line purpose | file |`, or update the doc's existing row (never a duplicate). Owner + Audience columns mandatory, match the doc's header `- Owner:` / `- Audience:`. A **retired** project's row lives in the `## Retired` section at the end of that file instead (move an existing row there) — see `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`. Bump the index's `Last updated` to today.
7b. **Link domain flows both ways** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`) — for each domain flow this project participates in, add a bullet in the project doc's **`## Flows` section** (`- **<Flow>** — [\`slug\`](../domain/slug.md)`) AND confirm the matching `domain/<slug>.md` lists this project under `Projects involved`; add it there if missing.
8. **Abstract repeats** — a fact that recurs in a 2nd project moves to {{#if has.observability_file}}`observability.md` / {{/if}}the reader `SKILL.md` (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §2`); the project doc keeps only its own value + a pointer.
9. **Show the diff, ask before committing.** Commit message: `knowledge: <project> — <short purpose>` (technical-only, no tool mentions).

## Pick a template

Pick by **deployment skeleton**, not by frontend/backend or language:

| Project type | Template | Notes |
|---|---|---|
{{#if has.skeleton_service}}
| **Deployed runtime** — backend API, frontend, message **consumer/worker**, scheduler | `templates/service.md` | Full section set; per-type differences marked inline (BACKEND / FRONTEND / CONSUMER / SCHEDULER). |
{{/if}}
{{#if has.skeleton_legacy_vm}}
| **VM-deployed app** (not containerized) | `templates/legacy-vm.md` | `Environments` → `Deployment` + per-env Hosts. |
{{/if}}
{{#if has.skeleton_artifact}}
| **Non-deployed artifact** — package library{{#if has.db_projects}}, DB-project{{/if}} | `templates/artifact.md` | No runtime sections (omit, don't write `none`). Keep type-specific: Packages / Publishing & usage (library){{#if has.db_projects}} · Databases / Deploy / Conventions & gotchas (DB-project){{/if}}. |
{{/if}}

{{#if has.skeleton_service}}
A **message consumer / worker** is a deployed runtime → `service.md` (NOT `artifact`): it has Environments / data stores / Observability — only its entry point is a queue, not HTTP.{{#if has.messaging}} Add the `<Send / process> chain` block.{{/if}}

{{/if}}
A project that fits no row above: ask the user which template to adapt, and keep the section order of `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §3`.

Sub-cases (apply on top of a template):
- **Sister / clone**: open with "clone of X, most conventions identical, this doc = differences only"; point shared parts back to X.
- **Family / group** (≥3 siblings sharing build/deploy/conventions): put the shared mechanics + a member index in a `<family>.md` overview doc; each member doc stays thin — its own values + a banner pointing to the family doc. Extract shared mechanics into the **family doc**, NOT into one member (a member is a peer, not the authority). See `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §2/§3`.
- **Multi-deployment one service**: list each deployment's responsibility + the split key (env var / reverse-proxy routing) + why it's split.
{{#if has.frontend}}
{{#if has.skeleton_service}}
- **Frontend** (in `service.md`): subdomain-prefix pattern only (not each env domain), the platform per env as a sub-bullet list, claim the render-chain flows it calls APIs for, client log structured (identifier + per-env), omitted when errors are only POSTed to the backend (say so under `logging`).
{{/if}}
{{/if}}

## Hard rules

See `CHECKLIST.md` for the actionable list; full rationale in `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`. The ones that caused real rework: ownership per `§6` (the owner source, else the user) · `- Audience:` header carries the internal/external classification (don't repeat "internal-only" in prose) · redact all secrets{{#if has.deployed_skeleton}} · env tables list only {{ lists.env_names }}{{#if has.excluded_envs}} (never {{ lists.env_excluded }}){{/if}}{{/if}}{{#if has.logs}} · verify log values with a live query, don't trust config{{/if}}.
